.TH PTY_REGION_COMPARE_STYLE 3 2026-10-03 "libptytest 0.1" "libptytest Manual"
.SH NAME
pty_region_compare_style, pty_region_style_map \- compare attributes and
colours of a region against a style map
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ptytest.h>
.PP
.B typedef struct {
.BR "    char key;" "   /* character used in style maps */"
.BR "    int  attr;" "  /* exact PTY_ATTR_* bitmask */"
.BR "    int  fg;" "    /* exact foreground colour */"
.BR "    int  bg;" "    /* exact background colour */"
.B } PtyStyleKey;
.PP
.BI "int pty_region_compare_style(PtySession *" s ", const PtyRegion *" reg ,
.BI "                             const char *const " expected "[],"
.BI "                             const PtyStyleKey " legend "[],"
.BI "                             char *" report ", size_t " report_size );
.BI "char *pty_region_style_map(PtySession *" s ", const PtyRegion *" reg ,
.BI "                           const PtyStyleKey " legend "[],"
.BI "                           char *" buf ", size_t " size );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Text tells what a screen says; style tells which line is selected, which
field has the error, which word is the title.
A style map states it as a picture: one ASCII character per cell, each
standing for a style defined in a
.IR legend ,
an array of
.I PtyStyleKey
ended by an entry whose
.I key
is 0.
A cell matches a key when its attributes and both colours are exactly
those of the key (see
.BR pty_cell_text (3)
for the colour values).
.PP
.BR pty_region_compare_style ()
compares the region
.I reg
(NULL: the whole screen; see
.BR pty_region_compare (3))
with the map
.IR expected .
Only the cells the map covers are checked: a map row may be shorter than
the region and the map may have fewer rows, which lets a test state the
style of the part that carries meaning and nothing else.
If the region's
.I wildcard
is a single ASCII character, it matches any style.
.PP
The report has the same shape as a text comparison's: a header, the
legend spelled out in words, a ruler, the expected (\(oq\-\(cq) and the
actual (\(oq+\(cq) map of each differing row, carets, and the row's text
under its maps so it is clear which word is styled wrongly.
In the actual map a style without a key is shown as \(oq#\(cq.
Notes name the differing columns, a styling shifted left or right by up to
three columns (the commonest highlight bug), and the first differing cell
with its character and both styles in words.
.PP
.BR pty_region_style_map ()
writes the actual map of a region in a legend, rows separated by newlines
\(em the starting point for a new expectation.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR pty_region_compare_style ()
returns 0 on a match, otherwise the number of differing rows, and \-1 if
the region is outside the screen, the map is larger than the region, a
map character is not in the legend, or an argument is NULL.
.BR pty_region_style_map ()
returns
.IR buf ,
empty for an invalid region.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
static const PtyStyleKey legend[] = {
    { \(aq.\(aq, PTY_ATTR_NONE,    PTY_FG_DEFAULT, PTY_BG_DEFAULT },
    { \(aqT\(aq, PTY_ATTR_BOLD,    PTY_FG_DEFAULT, PTY_BG_BLUE },
    { \(aqR\(aq, PTY_ATTR_REVERSE, PTY_FG_DEFAULT, PTY_BG_DEFAULT },
    { 0, 0, 0, 0 }
};
const char *const style[] = {
    ".TTTTTTTTTT.",
    ".RRRRRRRRRR.",
    NULL
};
ASSERT_REGION_STYLE(s, PTY_REGION(2, 2, 2, 12), style, legend);
.EE
.PP
A failure reads:
.PP
.EX
region "menu" rows 1\-1, cols 0\-11: style differs in 1 of 1 row
  (\- expected map, + actual map, ^ differing column, # style not in the legend)
  legend: .=plain; R=reverse
        0\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-1\-
\-    1 |.RRRRRR.|
+    1 |RRRRRR......|
        ^     ^
  text | item       |
notes:
  row 1: cols 0, 6 differ
    the styling is shifted left by 1 column
    first difference at col 0 (\(aq \(aq U+0020): expected . = plain, actual R = reverse
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pty_region_compare (3),
.BR pty_cell_text (3),
.BR pty_assert (3)
